WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:05.564
Although fewer than two hundred years have passed since the first man set foot on Antarctica, this frozen

2
00:00:05.565 --> 00:00:10.664
southern continent has been the center of a great deal of controversy. Countries have fought over the right

3
00:00:10.665 --> 00:00:17.512
to claim it as their own, military forces have hoped to use it as a stronghold, and modern

4
00:00:17.513 --> 00:00:22.003
businesses want to exploit its natural resources for profit. With so many competing interests at stake, Antarctica is

5
00:00:22.132 --> 00:00:29.217
carefully managed by a team of international legal experts. As a result, even though fewer than a

6
00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:34.834
thousand people actually make Antarctica their home, its frigid shores and vast glaciers have earned it a prominent

7
00:00:35.032 --> 00:00:42.103
position in the minds of people all over the world. An American seal hunter named John Davis was

8
00:00:42.321 --> 00:00:50.262
probably the first man to land on Antarctica, in 1821. Rumors of a southern landmass, however, had been

9
00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:55.530
circulating since antiquity. Aristotle believed that a lost continent would be discovered south of the "belt of fire,"

10
00:00:55.632 --> 00:01:02.529
his term for the equator. Nevertheless, for hundreds of years, expeditions around the Cape of Good Hope, at

11
00:01:02.652 --> 00:01:13.699
the foot of Africa, and Cape Horn, at the tip of South America, were unable to find land.

12
00:01:13.888 --> 00:01:23.416
Then finally, in the early nineteenth century, a series of naval ships from several different countries returned to

13
00:01:23.654 --> 00:01:32.273
their homes with news that they had sighted a continent. John Davis and his crew sailed south

14
00:01:32.608 --> 00:01:43.166
from their normal hunting grounds around the tip of Chile, and stepped onto the shores of the lost

15
00:01:43.575 --> 00:01:48.990
continent. Initially, the discovery was met with a flurry of excitement, but sailors soon recognized the extreme difficulties

16
00:01:49.221 --> 00:01:55.418
involved in navigating the icy waters around Antarctica. Temperatures in the region regularly dip to sixty degrees below

17
00:01:55.862 --> 00:01:59.887
zero Celsius during the winters, and ships can easily be trapped in the frigid oceans, held in place

18
00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.513
by slowly drifting icebergs. Traveling to Antarctica was not worth the risk to captain and crew. As a

19
00:02:04.514 --> 00:02:08.900
result, the continent was left alone for the rest of the century. Further exploration did not take place

20
00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:18.043
until the dawn of the twentieth century. British explorer Captain Robert F. Scott led an expedition that came

21
00:02:18.044 --> 00:02:21.962
within 770 kilometers of the South Pole in 1902. In 1907, the English sailor Ernest Shackleton led

22
00:02:21.963 --> 00:02:33.225
a team that covered approximately 2800 kilometers on foot before inclement weather forced them to turn back, only

23
00:02:33.226 --> 00:02:41.377
180 kilometers from the pole. Around the same time, several other expeditions from Britain and other European nations

24
00:02:41.378 --> 00:02:50.315
attempted to reach the pole, but failed. Ships were frequently icebound for months, and sailors often lost their

25
00:02:50.316 --> 00:02:55.130
sanity once the sun disappeared into the perpetual darkness of winter. In 1911, two more expeditions set out

26
00:02:55.131 --> 00:03:08.135
to reach the South Pole. The Norwegian explorer Roald Amundsen and his crew of five traveled by dogsled,

27
00:03:08.136 --> 00:03:15.024
surviving snow, wind, and frostbite to plant the Norwegian flag at the pole on December 14, 1911. When

28
00:03:15.025 --> 00:03:19.464
Robert Scott and his crew of six using horse-drawn sleds (which were much less successful than the dogsleds)

29
00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:23.605
arrived at the pole about a month later, on January 18, 1912, Amundsen’s flag told them they’d been

30
00:03:23.606 --> 00:03:30.704
beaten in their quest. Tragically, Scott and all of his men died of either starvation or injuries

31
00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:35.471
on their way back from the pole. Their bodies and Scott’s diary were found by a search party

32
00:03:35.472 --> 00:03:42.191
later that year. In 1914, Shackleton mounted another expedition, aboard a three-masted ship called the Endurance . Unfortunately,

33
00:03:42.192 --> 00:03:47.016
his vessel became caught in the ice before landing and was crushed. Forced to abandon the ship and

34
00:03:47.017 --> 00:03:56.158
camp on the ice for three months, the expedition survived only because Shackleton and five of the crew

35
00:03:56.356 --> 00:03:59.806
courageously sailed a lifeboat through 1300 kilometers of treacherous waters to a remote whaling station and organized a

36
00:03:59.889 --> 00:04:04.625
rescue that saved his crew. The near tragedy discouraged others from attempting a similar feat for another two

37
00:04:04.626 --> 00:04:10.161
decades. In fact, it wasn’t until World War II, as battles raged throughout Europe, Asia, and Africa, that

38
00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:20.498
the Antarctic region again became the center of international interest. Several explorations by airplane were undertaken with

39
00:04:20.499 --> 00:04:26.832
the aim of establishing a military base on the continent. But no country would relinquish its claim over

40
00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:31.046
the land to another. To pacify international relations and stimulate cooperation, all countries agreed to use the continent

41
00:04:31.047 --> 00:04:38.985
only for peaceful activities. Many protocols were drafted to ensure that no one could use the landmass as

42
00:04:38.986 --> 00:04:48.409
a military stronghold or exploit the land for nonrenewable resources. This work culminated in the drafting of the

43
00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:53.075
Antarctic Treaty of 1959, an international agreement that established a number of scientific outposts on the frozen continent,

44
00:04:53.076 --> 00:04:58.850
for the purposes of scientific research and the betterment of all humanity. The treaty has transformed Antarctica into

45
00:04:58.851 --> 00:05:03.175
a giant nature reserve, and placed it under the control of an international protection agency. Since that time,

46
00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:11.788
people wanting to gain access to the continent have had to seek permission from the agency. They must

47
00:05:11.927 --> 00:05:18.176
show that their presence in Antarctica will not damage the environment, and that they will share any

48
00:05:18.177 --> 00:05:26.377
scientific findings with the rest of the world. This, however, has not satisfied those who believe that Antarctica’s

49
00:05:26.378 --> 00:05:36.616
rich mineral and oil deposits should be explored and that commercial fishing should be allowed off its coasts.

50
00:05:36.617 --> 00:05:41.089
This has brought the continent back into the center of a number of global debates. Those who want

51
00:05:41.372 --> 00:05:48.534
to protect Antarctica argue that the continent plays a crucial role in the overall ecological health of the

52
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:55.598
world. Its ice sheet contains almost two-thirds of the planet’s supply of fresh water and is incredibly vulnerable

53
00:05:55.599 --> 00:06:05.972
to global warming. If the temperature at the South Pole increases as little as five degrees, glaciers will

54
00:06:06.085 --> 00:06:13.417
thaw, and the resulting meltwater could flood coastal cities all over the globe. Also, the water around Antarctica

55
00:06:13.418 --> 00:06:21.226
is a vital ecosystem for hundreds of species of fish, crustaceans, and algae. These organisms are the

56
00:06:21.227 --> 00:06:25.845
foundation of a massive food chain, and if their populations dwindle, Earth’s biodiversity will suffer. On the other

57
00:06:25.846 --> 00:06:28.956
hand, there are good arguments to support some commercial development. Antarctica is enormous and sparsely inhabited. Plants and

58
00:06:29.058 --> 00:06:34.844
animals, including humans, live only on its coasts, where the weather is less harsh. Mining companies contend that

59
00:06:34.845 --> 00:06:42.774
they can safely prospect for and extract minerals without disrupting the local ecosystem. Likewise, oil companies want to

60
00:06:42.945 --> 00:06:46.255
drill in Antarctica. They are concerned that the world’s reservoirs are being drained and that the continued prosperity

61
00:06:46.455 --> 00:06:54.589
of many economies depends on access to oil. Commercial fisheries are also interested in harvesting the oceans around

62
00:06:54.927 --> 00:06:59.697
Antarctica. While many see this as a good way to provide a cheap source of protein to feed

63
00:06:59.845 --> 00:07:09.019
the world’s growing population, others believe that it would be detrimental to the region. Some scientists express

64
00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:18.178
concern that fishing in the waters around Antarctica would upset the fragile balance of nature, causing the food

65
00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:26.241
pyramid to topple. With all of these issues in mind, the future of Antarctica, the first and only

66
00:07:26.459 --> 00:07:32.120
international continent, continues to inspire much debate. Whether one believes that it should remain unspoiled or that it

67
00:07:32.256 --> 00:07:35.889
is a resource ready to be exploited, the frozen southern continent is an essential part of the planet.

